+\subsubsection{BPA Fictive Contraction}\label{sec:fictive_contraction}
+
+The extensor configuration required one further correction, for a loss with a different physical origin than the compliance terms. As the knee flexes, the extensor BPA bends over the front of the joint and makes contact with it, so part of the actuator follows the joint surface instead of a straight line. Bent over the joint this way, the BPA behaves like a beam: the pressurized wall on the inside of the bend carries less tension than the wall on the outside, which both unbalances the actuator's force and consumes part of the wrapped arc length that would otherwise shorten the path. The wrapped section therefore stops contributing usable contraction length, and the actuator behaves as if it were more contracted than it physically is. This excess is termed fictive strain: the additional contraction the force model must see to reproduce the observed force, beyond the actuator's physical contraction. Figure~\ref{fig:xiProjection}\textbf{B} shows the wrapping geometry. A wrapping-loss term $\chi_{3}$ was introduced to scale the length that this fictive strain removes, such that
 \begin{equation}
     \Delta l = \begin{cases}
      \chi_{3}\;R\;\abs{ \theta_\text{wrap}-\theta_\text{k}}\;\zeta^2 & \theta_\text{k} \leq      \theta_\text{wrap} \\
-      0 & \theta_\text{k} \geq      \theta_\text{wrap}
+      0 & \theta_\text{k} \geq \theta_\text{wrap}
      \end{cases}\label{eq:deltaL}
 \end{equation}
-where $\Delta l$ is the change in usable length of the BPA, $R$ is the wrapping radius, $\abs{\theta_\text{wrap}-\theta_\text{k}}$ is the angle through which the BPA is bent once contact begins, and $\theta_\text{wrap}$, the knee flexion angle at which the BPA starts to bend over the joint, is determined from solid modeling. The factor $\zeta$ is the additive complement to the relative contraction,
-\begin{equation}
-    \zeta = 1-\varepsilon^*
+
+where $\Delta l$ is the change in usable length of the BPA, $R$ is the wrapping radius, $\abs{\theta_\text{wrap}-\theta_\text{k}}$ is the angle through which the BPA is bent once contact begins, and $\theta_\text{wrap}$ is the knee flexion angle at which the BPA starts to bend over the knee joint, determined from solid modeling. $\zeta$ is the additive complement to relative strain, such that
+
+\begin{equation}
+    \zeta = 1-\varepsilon^*  \\
 \label{eq:comp}
 \end{equation}
-so the wrapping loss vanishes at full contraction and grows quadratically toward resting length. Combining Equations~\eqref{eq:comp} and~\eqref{eq:deltaL} with the offset of Equation~\eqref{eq:Lm} yields the fully corrected muscle length
+
+so the wrapping loss grows quadratically as the actuator approaches its resting length and vanishes at full contraction. Combining \eqref{eq:comp} into \eqref{eq:deltaL}, and subtracting $\Delta l$ from  \eqref{eq:Lm} yields
+
 \begin{equation}
