-\end{figure}
-
-The direct extensor route exceeds the human envelope in magnitude at every angle, with zero shortfall points. Across the design range, the predicted two-BPA torque spans \qty{35.45}{\newton\meter} to \qty{109.4}{\newton\meter} against a human-scaled requirement of \qty{20.25}{\newton\meter} to \qty{50.32}{\newton\meter}, and the required overshoot is consumed as the thinnest margin exactly as designed, $+5.02$~\% at the worst-case angle of \ang{-20.2}. The route respects the wrap-release schedule of the route-elimination rule, releasing its six sequential contacts at knee angles of \ang{-89.8}, \ang{-55.7}, \ang{-47.8}, \ang{-20.2}, \ang{6.1}, and \ang{7.4}, and the optimized design places the femur-side origin at $(40.0, 137.0, 0)$~mm and the tibial insertion at $(34.4, -197.1, 0)$~mm, with a resting length of \qty{827.7}{\mm} and \qty{36.8}{\mm} of tendon.
-
-\begin{figure}[H]
-\begin{center}
-\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{Figures/ExtensorRoute_redesigned.eps}
-\end{center}
-\caption{Route geometry of the cam extensor design of record, drawn in the femur frame. Panels \textbf{(A)}--\textbf{(H)} show eight evenly spaced knee poses from full flexion ($\theta_\text{k} = \ang{-120}$) to full extension ($\theta_\text{k} = \ang{10}$); knee angle is negative in flexion. Each panel draws the optimized route and its cam contacts over the femur-cylinder, femur-wall, condyle, and tibia clearance envelopes of the route-elimination rule, with the local bend-radius checks dashed; the square marker is the femur-side origin, the diamond marker is the tibial insertion, and the ninth tile carries the legend and the contact-release schedule.}\label{fig:ExtensorRoute_redesigned}
-\end{figure}
-
-The extensor route was then redesigned once more by allowing the tibial-side route points to trace a cam profile, and this cam redesign is the current extensor design. The cam converts the razor-thin margin of the direct route into genuine headroom. On the final geometry, which folds the optimizer winner together with a verified adjustment of the distal route point and tendon length, the minimum torque margin is $+30.83$~\% with respect to the required envelope and $+39.77$~\% with respect to the plain human target; the unadjusted optimizer solution carried $+31.30$~\% and $+42.34$~\% at the same checks. The pressure required to meet the envelope spans \qty{269}{\kPa} to \qty{507}{\kPa} over the range of motion, comfortably above the \qty{150}{\kPa} regulated floor of the valve manifold that supplies the hardware, so the remaining pressure is a design resource. The geometry confirms that the headroom is not bought with routing risk: the tibial wrap follows a bend radius of \qty{96.9}{\mm}, the guiding route point clears the femoral-condyle envelope by \qty{43.1}{\mm} at \ang{10} of knee extension, and the moment arm spans \qty{59.4}{\mm} to \qty{102.8}{\mm} with a mean of \qty{72.4}{\mm} (Figure~\ref{fig:ExtensorRoute_redesigned}).
-
-The flexor redesign was a two-BPA mirrored-route solution rather than a single shared route. The first actuator's route is carried directly as design variables, its origin on the femur and its insertion on the tibia, and the second actuator's endpoints are solved from it by a mirrored-endpoint rule: the second insertion mirrors the first across the mid-sagittal plane, and the second origin is placed so that the second route spans the same origin-to-insertion offset as the first, which keeps the two routes from crossing. Each route is then rebuilt by the route solver, so the second carries its own wrap point and its own tibial and femoral-condyle clearance state rather than inheriting the first's, with condyle clearance enforced in the nonlinear constraints. In the design of record the first route clears the tibial envelope at every angle and never wraps, while the second rides its standoff only within about \ang{5} of full extension before the wrap releases. The design meets the flexor target with a minimum margin fraction of 0.0501 against the required 0.05. Its predicted knee-axis torque spans \qty{-81.23}{\newton\meter} to \qty{-3.864}{\newton\meter} against the human-scaled requirement of \qty{-23.16}{\newton\meter} to \qty{-2.461}{\newton\meter} (peak magnitude \qty{23.16}{\newton\meter}), again exceeding the envelope at every angle. The flexor prediction applies the adopted correction terms, including the wrapping factor identified on the extensor configuration: where a route's wrap is active, the actuator loses usable length through the same wrapping mechanism, and the extensor-identified $\chi_{3}$ was applied to it unchanged, a transfer whose testing remains future work (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).
-
-The bi-pulley machinery was then applied as a batch over five anatomical muscle sites of the robot-body model's leg, covering both halves of the pulley doctrine: three uniarticular sites that the mono-pulley variant serves, soleus and tibialis anterior at the ankle and biceps femoris short head at the knee, and two biarticular sites that the bi-pulley variant serves, medial gastrocnemius across the knee and ankle and rectus femoris across the hip and knee. Every run of record operated at unity transmission, one BPA and one tackle line part per crossing, with the tendon exit on the moving-force-point routing; the bowden-housing arrangement is implemented in the machinery but was never exercised. Because the point of a reeved tackle is to exchange actuator force for range of motion, each site was then re-solved in full at three doctrine configurations of the transmission: a parallel pair of BPAs through a two-part tackle (force gain 1, travel gain 2), the same pair through a four-part tackle (force gain 0.5, travel gain 4), and a four-actuator group through a four-part tackle (force gain 1, travel gain 4), all at supply pressures of \qtylist{300;450;620}{\kPa}. The actuator sizes are re-solved rather than carried over, because a route sized for direct drive runs its tendon slack under a multi-part tackle that feeds takeup faster than the span demands; the optimizer re-fits the actuator lengths to the transmission at every configuration. The resulting torque surfaces at the two-actuator, two-part configuration were evaluated over joint angle and supply pressure at each site's evaluation plane: soleus and tibialis anterior over the ankle, biceps femoris short head over the knee, medial gastrocnemius over the knee with the ankle pinned at zero, and rectus femoris over the hip of its recorded route; the margins against the human requirements were likewise evaluated over joint angle along the transmission ladder at \qty{620}{\kPa}. The human requirements are maximum-isometric torques of the stock Gait2392 model \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2018}, computed as OpenSim moment arms times maximum isometric force (\qty{3549}{\N} soleus, \qty{905}{\N} tibialis anterior, \qty{1558}{\N} medial gastrocnemius, \qty{1169}{\N} rectus femoris, and \qty{804}{\N} biceps femoris short head), all five sites on the same OpenSim basis.
-
-The re-solves show the exchange operating in the direction the doctrine intends: at matched net force, the meeting range against the human requirement grows with the tackle's travel gain. The tibialis-anterior route is the strongest site: the single-actuator baseline meets the human dorsiflexor requirement, which spans \qtyrange{24.60}{39.35}{\newton\meter} over the ankle range, across \ang{48.1} of the \ang{70} ankle range (\ang{46.7} contiguous) at \qty{620}{\kPa} with a peak of \qty{52.0}{\newton\meter}, and its meeting range grows from zero at \qty{300}{\kPa} to \ang{14.6} at \qty{450}{\kPa} and \ang{48.1} at \qty{620}{\kPa}; the parallel pair through the two-part tackle holds the peak at \qty{53.0}{\newton\meter} and extends the meeting range to \ang{56.9} (\ang{55.4} contiguous), and the four-actuator group through the four-part tackle extends it further to \ang{59.8} (\ang{58.3} contiguous). At the knee, the biceps femoris short head is already served over its whole range by the direct two-actuator flexor redesign of Section~\ref{sec:route_redesign}, which exceeds its requirement at every angle of the full \ang{130} span at a minimum margin fraction of 0.0501; the transmission study at this site therefore starts from the batch's unity-transmission single-actuator run, which meets its OpenSim-graded requirement at no angle, with eight of twenty-five grid cells infeasible at every pressure. Against that single-actuator baseline, the matched-force pair removes every infeasible cell and meets over \ang{21.7} of the span at a peak of \qty{34.1}{\newton\meter}, and the four-actuator group reaches \ang{40.6}; at this site the tackle's contribution is the multiplied insertion travel and the actuator-count scalability it buys, not meeting range that the direct design lacks. The medial gastrocnemius baseline meets over \ang{21.7} of the knee sweep with seven of twenty-five cells infeasible; the matched-force pair extends the meeting range to \ang{65.0}, and at force gain 0.5, with the pair alone feeding the four-part tackle, the design meets over \ang{105.6} of the sweep at a peak of \qty{118.0}{\newton\meter}, while the four-actuator group at matched force meets over \ang{119.2} at a peak of \qty{236.1}{\newton\meter}. The rectus femoris route was recorded with its knee crossing rigid, so its margins are evaluated over the hip angle, where the baseline meets over \ang{46.9} at \qty{620}{\kPa} and the matched-force pair more than doubles the meeting range to \ang{99.0}. The soleus site is the honest limit of the exchange: it never meets its human requirement at any transmission, a peak of \qty{25.3}{\newton\meter} against a required magnitude of \qtyrange{134.08}{171.35}{\newton\meter}, a factor of 6.8 short, because its shortfall is in force, and a tackle that divides force cannot add it, only the actuator count can.
-
-Physical validation of the redesigned extensor route is under way on the rebuilt cam route with the \qty{20}{\mm} actuator, and the first full angle sweep is complete. An initial three-point hold near full extension on the earlier direct route measured knee torque of \qty{1.163}{\newton\meter} at \ang{10}, \qty{7.849}{\newton\meter} at \ang{11}, and \qty{23.437}{\newton\meter} at \ang{10}, against hung loads of \qtylist{3.7;25.2;76}{\newton} at gauge pressures of \qtylist{388.5;388;592}{\kPa}, with the measured wrench carried to the moving knee center by the same adjoint transform as the identification data. On the rebuilt cam route the sweep then held six isometric points from deep flexion to extension: \qty{116}{\newton} hung at \ang{-119} and \qty{256}{\kPa}, \qty{168}{\newton} at \ang{-61} and \qty{425}{\kPa}, \qty{181}{\newton} at \ang{-43} and \qty{435}{\kPa}, \qty{180.2}{\newton} at \ang{-26.2} and \qty{437}{\kPa}, \qty{181.5}{\newton} at \ang{-30} and \qty{420}{\kPa}, and \qty{100}{\newton} at \ang{5} and \qty{480}{\kPa}, each with its load-cell angle recorded in the tibia frame. The post-test rig measurement of the reaction-point geometry is complete, and the adjoint reduction of the six points follows in Figure~\ref{fig:Ext20mm}. The corresponding flexor validation is reported with the biomimetic flexor results (Figure~\ref{fig:FlxGrp}\textbf{C}).
-
-The pressures alone already read against the design. The deep-flexion hold at \ang{-119} was carried at \qty{256}{\kPa}, at the low end of the cam design's predicted \qtyrange{269}{507}{\kPa} requirement band, and the extension hold at \ang{5} at \qty{480}{\kPa} against the \qty{507}{\kPa} worst case, so the route held loads across the full range without approaching the \qty{620}{\kPa} ceiling.
-
-With the reaction-point constants entered, the six holds reduce to knee-extension torques of \qty{30.24}{\newton\meter} at \ang{-119}, \qty{37.98}{\newton\meter} at \ang{-61}, \qty{38.30}{\newton\meter} at \ang{-43}, \qty{37.23}{\newton\meter} at \ang{-26.2}, \qty{38.84}{\newton\meter} at \ang{-30}, and \qty{22.86}{\newton\meter} at \ang{5}, with the flexor-template adjoint sign convention flipped so that extension torque reads positive (Figure~\ref{fig:Ext20mm}). Against the corrected prediction of the cam design evaluated at the measured pressures, the holds agree to within \qty{6.6}{\newton\meter} at five of the six angles and exceed it by \qty{5.26}{\newton\meter} at \ang{-119}. Against the maximum-isometric vasti target at the held angles, only the deep-flexion hold at \ang{-119} meets the human target, by \qty{1.24}{\newton\meter}, while the remaining five fall short by \qtyrange{5.0}{11.9}{\newton\meter} at supply pressures of \qtyrange{256}{480}{\kPa}, below the \qty{620}{\kPa} design pressure at which the predicted curve exceeds the target at every held angle.
