-\subsection{Design Factors for Isometric Torque}\label{sec:design_factors}
-
-Read as a design problem, isometric torque about a BPA-actuated joint factors into three quantities the designer controls: the moment arm $r$, the normalized contraction $\varepsilon^{*}$ at which the actuator operates, and the compliance of the path and its fixtures, carried by the $\chi$ terms. Each result in this paper maps onto one corner of that triangle. Short moment arms amplify every error, so stiffness must be budgeted where the arm is short \citep{young_analyzing_2019}. For a given $l_{0}$, moment arm and contraction allowance trade against each other, and the cam redesign shows the trade can be favorable: it raises the moment arm from the direct route's \qty{44.9}{\mm} to \qty{61.2}{\mm} to a span of \qty{59.4}{\mm} to \qty{102.8}{\mm} while improving the torque margin. This increased moment arm comes at a cost to biomimcry.  Pressure headroom, finally, is a design resource: the cam route meets its envelope between \qty{269}{\kPa} and \qty{507}{\kPa} against a \qty{620}{\kPa} ceiling.
-
-These factors situate our work among musculoskeletal robot legs. Multifilament muscle routing has produced human-scale lower limbs \citep{kurumaya_musculoskeletal_2016}, anthropomorphic arms \citep{hitzmann_anthropomorphic_2018}, and full human-mimetic humanoids \citep{asano_musculoskeletal_2019}, and tendon-driven bodies have supported investigations from reflexive hopping \citep{liu_robotic_2018} to human-like manipulation \citep{chen_realizing_2019} and cognitive locomotion \citep{schilling_adaptive_2022}. Linkage-based knees manage the moment-arm problem geometrically \citep{romero_four-bar_2020}, reviews of dynamic multilegged robots treat actuation routing as a first-order design decision \citep{he_mechanism_2020}, and tunable-compliance actuators make fixture compliance a design element rather than an error source \citep{leibach_development_2020}. What these designs share with ours is the recognition that the attachment route, not the actuator alone, sets the deliverable torque. What the corrected model adds is a quantitative accounting of the compliance and wrapping losses along that route, so that a route can be designed against a torque target rather than checked after the fact. \citet{morrow_optimization_2020} optimized artificial-muscle placement on a bipedal robot with a rigid-body torque model, and the correction terms quantified here are the physics such rigid-body placements leave unmodeled.
-
-The correction family also complements the BPA force-modeling literature. Static models of the actuator in isolation, from the classical McKibben analyses \citep{chou_measurement_1996} through approximation formulas \citep{sarosi_new_2012} to geometrically grounded models of the Festo muscle \citep{martens_modeling_2017, tondu_static_2022}, describe the actuator itself; none of them carries attachment compliance, length offsets, or wrapping losses into a joint-torque prediction. The terms validated here attach to the force characterization of \citet{bolen_2026} and travel with it across joint configurations under one consistent parameter set.
-
-\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}\label{sec:limitations}
-
- The correction terms are identified for this test stand, these knee configurations, and these actuator families. Other joints or actuator types should repeat the two-stage procedure rather than reuse the values. That procedure is inexpensive next to the test campaign it saves, which is the point of having it.
-
- Isometric torque testing has yielded important design factors for roboticists seeking to design biomimetic humanoid robots. Additional testing such as isokinetic and isotonic that are frequently used in in human dynamometer testing may yield equally as important insights.
-
-\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
-
-This work started with an improved braided pneumatic actuator force-length-pressure model and actually delivers about a joint, and answered with a corrected torque model and the design workflow built on it. Isometric knee-torque measurements on pinned and biomimetic test stands, interpreted through a hybrid calculation that substitutes the measured muscle length and moment arm, isolated the error sources that rigid-body torque models miss: attachment compliance, a constant effective-length offset, and a wrapping loss that acts like fictive strain, expressed through the unloaded \qty{620}{\kPa} contraction of the free actuator. A two-stage identification recovered the correction terms with one stiffness pair shared across the flexor and extensor configurations, roughly halving fitting-set error, cutting held-out error by two thirds or more, and carrying the corrected model from the pinned knee to the biomimetic knee without refitting. The attachment routes themselves were then redesigned under the corrected model: two $\diameter$\qty{20}{\mm} actuators per route, graded against OpenSim human isometric torque profiles with a required \qty{5}{\percent} overshoot, meet or exceed the human flexor and extensor envelopes at every angle, the cam extensor design converts the thinnest margins into design headroom, the doctrine re-solves quantify how a reeved tackle, fed by parallel actuator pairs, converts surplus force into range of motion across five muscle sites, and the first physical torque points of the redesigned extensor route have been measured near full extension. The design-factor reading is the practical summary: moment arm, normalized contraction, and path compliance are the three quantities a designer of BPA-actuated joints controls, and each now carries a measurable, correctable model. First-iteration torque design for such joints is, as a result, no longer a guess.
