+\end{table} What the correction terms do to the prediction is quantified in Tables~\ref{tab:FlxPinGof} and~\ref{tab:ExtPinGof}. The corrected flexor model holds FVU at or below 0.18 on the four tests of the identification, the worst case being the \qty{48.5}{\cm} training test at 0.172, and the post hoc evaluation of the unused \qty{47.9}{\cm} test improves as well (Appendix~\ref{app:model_geometry}). On the extensor identification the corrected model improves the RMSE on every used test, cutting the training-triple mean from \qty{2.50}{\newton\cdot\meter} to \qty{1.00}{\newton\cdot\meter} and the validation-triple mean from \qty{2.55}{\newton\cdot\meter} to \qty{1.10}{\newton\cdot\meter}, and holds FVU below unity on every used test except the \qty{43.2}{\cm} tendon test. On the biomimetic extensor test ($l_{0} = \qty{51.8}{\cm}$) the corrected prediction improves RMSE from \qty{3.03}{\newton\cdot\meter} to \qty{1.17}{\newton\cdot\meter} and FVU from 4.49 to 0.67, so the shared-pair model explains the bulk of that configuration's measured torque variance as well as its level. The genetic multiobjective solver is retained for the identification, where the Pareto front is required for holdout validation, and the surrogate-plus-pattern-search pipeline serves the route redesign (Section~\ref{sec:optimization}).
+
+The initial route searches bounded the BPA origins and insertions near the corresponding human muscle attachments to preserve biomimetic routing. Those anatomical bounds had to be relaxed where the available BPA contraction could not cover the knee range of motion. The resulting routes then required a second tradeoff: reducing the actuator forces carried by the attachment hardware after parts failed during testing. The cam redesign changes the moment arm and contact geometry to retain the torque requirement at lower actuator force.
+
+The route redesign built on these values addresses the single-muscle shortfall at high flexion angles documented in Section~\ref{sec:results_torque}: each configuration now carries two \qty{20}{\mm} BPAs per route. The flexor solution meets the flexor torque target with a minimum margin of $+5.01$\,\% at $-107^{\circ}$, with a separately solved route for each actuator. The second insertion mirrors the first across the sagittal plane, while its origin preserves the first route's lateral endpoint offset; its wrapping and clearance are evaluated independently. The direct extensor route meets or exceeds its human-scaled target with the required 5\,\% overshoot as the thinnest margin at every angle, $+5.02$\,\% at worst, while respecting the wrap-release schedule and contraction limits of the route-elimination rule (Appendix~\ref{app:model_geometry}); it was later superseded by the cam redesign of Section~\ref{sec:route_redesign_results}. These are mechanical design predictions. Their complete incorporation into the converted neuromechanical models remains future integration work.
+
+\subsubsection{Redesigned-Route Results on the Gait2392 Robot-Body Model}\label{sec:route_redesign_results}
+
+This subsection records the quantitative outcomes of the redesigned routes on the Gait2392-derived robot-body model, whose muscle force and torque targets define the redesign objectives. All three result families were produced by the surrogate-plus-pattern-search pipeline of Section~\ref{sec:optimization} under the adopted correction terms (Appendix~\ref{app:model_geometry}, Table~\ref{tab:app_xi_values}) with one shared actuator plan: two \qty{20}{\mm} BPAs per route, a \qty{620}{\kPa} pressure ceiling, and a maximum usable contraction of 0.255 in the force model. Each family minimizes its own objective function, a unitless weighted sum of normalized penalty terms in which zero corresponds to no violated penalty, led by the worst-angle and mean-square torque shortfall against the required 5\,\%-overshoot human envelope, followed by overshoot, rest-length, strain, contraction, and mild design-change penalties. The three functions differ in penalty families and normalizations (the flexor and pulley objectives add off-axis-excess, wrap-release, and second-BPA anchor terms, and the pulley objective evaluates its torque requirement on the insertion-side torque after the tackle's force loss), so their numerical values are not commensurable with one another. The human-scaled requirements are the OpenSim isometric torque profiles of the reference human muscles over the same design-grid angles, the vastus medialis for the extensor and the biceps femoris short head for the flexor.
+
+The direct extensor route exceeds the human envelope in magnitude at every angle, with zero shortfall points: across the design range the predicted two-BPA extensor torque spans \qty{35.45}{\newton\cdot\meter} to \qty{109.4}{\newton\cdot\meter} against the human-scaled requirement of \qty{20.25}{\newton\cdot\meter} to \qty{50.32}{\newton\cdot\meter}, and the required 5\,\% overshoot is consumed as the thinnest margin exactly as designed, at a minimum margin of $+5.02$\,\% at the worst-case angle of $-20.2^{\circ}$.
